{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== THE AMENDED PLAN IS ALSO WRONG, AND IT WOULD SHIP DATA LOSS. STOP BEFORE IMPLEMENTING. ===\n\nI did not start the implementation. Verifying the plan's one load-bearing premise refuted it, in the\nsame class as the first failure: an inference about what `managed_refs` MEANS, stated in the plan and\nnever traced to the code.\n\n--- THE PREMISE, AND WHY IT IS FALSE ---\n\nThe amended G4 rule is: \"ref IS managed + peer lacks it -> the peer DELETED it -> inbound REMOVE wins\nand the outbound ADD is suppressed.\" The plan justifies its safety with: \"an UNMANAGED dep is never\nremoved (the outbound ADD wins), so a brand-new local dep is safe by construction.\"\n\n**A brand-new local dep is NOT unmanaged.** The plan says so itself, two paragraphs earlier, and the\ntwo statements contradict each other: `add_managed_ref` is folded by the LINK-event processor\n(`reducer/_processors.py:411`, inside `process_link`), so a ref is managed THE INSTANT IT IS CREATED\nLOCALLY. `should_propagate_removal` (`_managed_refs.py:140-153`) is nothing but\n`(kind, target) in managed_ref_set(local_ticket)` — no push, no peer, no history.\n\nSo the residual window the plan describes as narrow (\"chiefly `seed_managed_refs_from_current`, a\nPRE-FEATURE ticket's current deps\") is not narrow at all. It is EVERY locally created link, from the\nmoment of creation until it is pushed.\n\n--- MEASURED, NOT REASONED (probe run against gerrit/main @ b6916709f3) ---\n\nDrove the reducer and the production differ directly:\n\n  after a LOCAL link event, managed_refs = [['blocks', 'local-b']]\n  should_propagate_removal('blocks','local-b') = True\n\n  (a) _diff_link_removals_inbound, brand-new never-pushed dep, issuelinks observed []\n        -> [{'action': 'remove', 'target_id': 'local-b', 'relation': 'blocks'}]\n  (b) compute_inbound_mutations, no outbound context\n        -> [{'action': 'remove', 'target_id': 'local-b', 'relation': 'blocks'}]\n  (c) compute_inbound_mutations, WITH the outbound link ADD a real bidirectional pass emits\n        -> []\n\nRead (c) against (b): **the landed G4 outbound-add suppression is the ONLY thing standing between a\nbrand-new local link and its deletion.** The amended plan's central instruction is to delete that\nsuppression. Doing so turns a dormant feature into an active data-loss bug on the ORDINARY CREATE\nPATH: a human creates a link locally, the first reconcile pass emits an inbound REMOVE (managed +\npeer lacks it), the arbitration suppresses the outbound ADD, and the link is destroyed instead of\npushed. That is strictly worse than today's no-op.\n\n--- WHY NO PATCH TO THE RULE CAN FIX THIS (the part that decides the ticket) ---\n\nThe two scenarios are STATE-IDENTICAL:\n  * a brand-new local link never pushed to the peer, and\n  * a link rebar pushed that the peer then deleted\nboth present exactly `managed = True`, `target bound = True`, `issuelinks observed = True`, `peer link\nabsent = True`. Every input the guards read is the same in both. Therefore ANY arbitration that is a\nfunction of (managed, bound, observed, current peer set) MUST return the same answer for both, and one\nof those answers is data loss. This is not a tuning problem; it is a missing-signal problem.\n\nThe discriminator can only be HISTORY: \"was this link ever on the peer\" or \"did we ever push it\".\nNeither is persisted today. `should_propagate_removal` cannot supply it; that is the whole finding.\n\nNote what this means for the existing green test `test_peer_deleted_link_emits_a_removal`: its input\nis the brand-new-link state. It is named for one scenario and pins both. That is the same failure the\nfirst version had — the oracle asserts the specification, and the specification cannot see the\ndifference.\n\n--- WHAT THE LANDED CODE (1276) ACTUALLY DOES TODAY, stated plainly ---\n\nThe feature is dormant and the pass is SAFE. A link deleted in Jira is re-created by the outbound ADD\non the next pass: local-wins, convergent, no local data loss, and the human's Jira-side deletion is\nsilently reverted. That is a real divergence from the AC, and it FAILS SAFE.\n\n--- THE ONE CORRECT DESIGN I CAN SEE, AND ITS COST ---\n\nArbitrate on the PREVIOUS PEER LINK SET: link present in the prior pass's peer snapshot AND absent now\n= a real remote deletion; absent in both = never pushed, so the outbound ADD wins. Brand-new deps are\nthen safe BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by a gate, and `managed_refs` drops out of the arbitration\nentirely (it can stay as a belt-and-braces gate).\n\nThe plan rejected this as \"a new persisted artifact with its own migration and truncation semantics\".\nThat cost is lower than stated, and the ticket should be re-planned knowing it:\n  * the artifact ALREADY EXISTS and is already written and committed every pass —\n    `_write_prev_snapshot_key_set` (`reconcile.py:538-541`) writes `{jira_key: {}}` to\n    `.bridge_state/prev.json`. This is one added field per key, not a new file.\n  * migration is trivially fail-safe: an old `prev.json` has no link data, so nothing is provable, so\n    NO removal fires; it self-heals after one pass.\n  * truncation is fail-safe on both legs: current-pass enrichment missing -> G1 already declines;\n    prior-pass enrichment missing -> presence unprovable -> decline.\n  * the narrowing was deliberate (2cc0-da4a-d736-4ba2, \"Narrow persisted Jira snapshot to key\n    membership ... while RETAINING FULL PER-PASS SNAPSHOTS\") and was about DETERMINISM of the\n    persisted prev state. Widening it re-opens that question and must answer it, not ignore it — but\n    note the full per-pass snapshots are retained on disk, so the raw signal is already being captured.\n\n--- STATUS ---\n\nImplementation NOT started, deliberately. The certified plan cannot be implemented as written without\nshipping the defect above, and `review-plan` certified it because the plan is internally consistent\nabout a false premise — the gate reads the plan, not the reducer.\n\nAwaiting the operator's call on build-the-history-signal vs. leave-the-feature-dormant-and-document.\nWhichever is chosen, the plan needs a fresh amendment and a fresh review round; and per this ticket's\nown AC, the live cell is the arbiter, so nothing here is settled by a green unit suite."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785771190587563001,"uuid":"7e08556c-a2ee-4588-9432-f860c290dea0"}